+### Connection age
+
+gRPC clients keep a connection for as long as it works and usually re-resolve
+the server's name only when a connection closes. Behind a DNS name that
+resolves to several receivers (for example a Kubernetes headless Service with
+clients using `dns:///` and `round_robin`), a receiver added later gets no
+traffic from clients that are already connected. `max_connection_age` fixes
+this the way grpc-go's `MaxConnectionAge` does:
+
+- When a connection reaches `max_connection_age` (each connection's age is
+  jittered by +/-10%, so connections opened together do not reconnect
+  together), the receiver sends a graceful HTTP/2 GOAWAY: first with the last
+  stream id 2^31-1 and a PING, then, one round trip later, with the id of the
+  last stream it accepted. The client starts new RPCs on a new connection,
+  re-resolving the name, while the RPCs already in flight finish on the old
+  one, which then closes.
+- `max_connection_age_grace` bounds how long those RPCs may take. When it runs
+  out the connection is closed under them: they fail without a response, so
+  the client cannot tell whether they were processed and resends them. Set it
+  above `timeout`, so that an RPC still running at the GOAWAY always gets its
+  response first; the receiver logs a warning at startup otherwise.
+- Both default to `null` (connections live forever). The grace requires the
+  age, and the age must be greater than zero.
+
